Problem Framing
The startup must select the best fraud detection model to minimize total monthly cost, balancing false negatives (missed fraud) and false positives (incorrectly blocked legitimate transactions). The business constraint is asymmetric:

False Negative (FN) cost = ₹50,000 per fraud missed
False Positive (FP) cost = ₹500 per legitimate transaction blocked

With ~57,000 transactions/month and ~98 actual fraud cases, the decision hinges on precision–recall trade-offs, with recall being critical due to the high cost of missed fraud.

Analysis (with Cost Estimates)
We estimate counts using:

TP = Recall × 98
FN = 98 − TP
FP = TP × (1 / Precision − 1)


Model A (No adjustment)

Recall = 63% → TP ≈ 62, FN ≈ 36
Precision = 82% → FP ≈ 14

Model A cost:
(14 × 500) + (36 × 50,000)
= 7,000 + 1,800,000
= ₹1,807,000


Model B (Class-balanced weights)

Recall = 91% → TP ≈ 89, FN ≈ 9
Precision = 6% → FP ≈ 1,395

Model B cost:
(1,395 × 500) + (9 × 50,000)
= 697,500 + 450,000
= ₹1,147,500


Model C (Random undersampling)

Recall = 91% → TP ≈ 89, FN ≈ 9
Precision = 3% → FP ≈ 2,877

Model C cost:
(2,877 × 500) + (9 × 50,000)
= 1,438,500 + 450,000
= ₹1,888,500


Model D (SMOTE 10% ratio)

Recall = 88% → TP ≈ 86, FN ≈ 12
Precision = 35% → FP ≈ 160

Model D cost:
(160 × 500) + (12 × 50,000)
= 80,000 + 600,000
= ₹680,000
